📗
  🛃 A guard: who may call a route, by a note on the route and the token the
  request brings.

    🆕🛃 tokens❗️ ➡️ guard
    🧱 app 🛃 app guard❗️❗️

    🔖 📥 app 🔤/admin/stats🔤 stats❗️ 🔤auth🔤 🔤role:admin🔤❗️

  A route's note auth says who may call it: anyone; signed-in, which is
  whoever brings a token that holds; role:NAME, a token whose roles has NAME;
  scope:NAME, the same of its scope. A route without the note is open to
  anyone, unless the guard was told 🚷. The token comes in the request's
  Authorization header, as "Bearer" and the token. Every setter returns the
  guard.

  What a token that holds says is put into the request's bag for the layers
  and the handler behind the guard: auth.subject, auth.roles, auth.scope, and
  auth.claims, the claims as JSON text. The roles and the scope are names
  with one space between them. A name has no white space in it: what a token
  lists that has is no name, and is left out.
